 "result": "The exception argument comes from three places:\n\n- **Call site: `src/tessel/app.py:1472-1474`.** `run_app_teardowns` loops over `on_app_teardown_funcs` in reverse and calls each as `func(exc)`. It uses the same `exc` it received, which is `None` by default (`app.py:1453-1455`). The `appcontext_tearing_down` signal gets the same `exc` at line 1477.\n- **Where `exc` is chosen: `src/tessel/ctx.py:496`.** `AppContext.pop(exc)` forwards its own `exc` to `run_app_teardowns`. It does this only once `_push_count` reaches 0 (lines 481-484), so nested pushes don't trigger teardown.\n- **Who passes it to `pop`: `ctx.py:510`.** `__exit__` is the place to check. It should pass `exc_value`, so a `with` block that raised gives the function an exception and a clean exit gives `None`. Other callers of `.pop(` may pass nothing, which also gives `None`.\n\nIf a teardown function sometimes gets `None` when you expect an exception, check whether the code that pops the context uses `with` or calls `pop()` directly without an `exc`. An exception that was already handled (for example by an error handler) also won't reach it.\n\n`tests/test_appctx.py` (around lines 47-230) has concrete cases of teardown functions and the arguments they receive.",
